{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-47053",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since $f$ is a continuous function on $[-1,8]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,8].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,8]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $f(-1)=-5$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-2\\in [-5,0],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,2],$ we have $f(0)=0$ and $f(2)=4.$ Since $-2\\notin [0,4],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,4],$ we have $f(2)=4$ and $f(4)=-1.$ Since $-2\\notin [-1,4],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[4,5],$ we have $f(4)=-1$ and $f(5)=-4.$ Since $-2\\in [-4,-1],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[5,8],$ we have $f(5)=-4$ and $f(8)=8.$ Since $-2\\in [-4,8],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $f(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0],$ $[4,5],$ and $[5,8].$ Therefore, $f(x)=-2$ has at least three solutions.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
